What it does: it predicts the component's activation from the newest token alone.
- A Chinese, Japanese or Korean word (CJK characters) gets a fairly large positive value.
- An English (ASCII) word gets a small positive value that grows with its length:
  short words such as "the" or "of" are near 0, long content words are clearly positive.
- Digits, punctuation, whitespace and partial UTF-8 bytes get 0.

Why: the component almost never passes the causal-importance threshold (0 of 10M tokens)
and it barely changes the model's output when switched off, so this is a description of a
small, diffuse signal, not of a circuit. Its largest activations are on specific words
(e.g. gambling, beauty, rumour, abortion, peasant words) and its most negative ones on code
identifiers and word fragments; those token-specific effects are not captured by the
length/script rule here. A lookup table of per-token means would explain far more on
held-out text (variance explained about 0.3), but it is a table with no concept behind it.
